## What is formally proven

Under the supplied `MPY` semantics, for arbitrary symbolic floats `A`, `B`,
and `T`, if

```k
floatLt(absF(subF(A, B)), T)
```

holds, executing the exact `has_close_elements` closure on
`list(vCons(A, vCons(B, .ValSeq)))` returns `true`.

This is a partial-correctness theorem. The three positive claims cover:

- the inner loop at `i = 0`, `j = 1`, and `n = 2`;
- the outer loop at `i = 0`, `j = 0`, and `n = 2`;
- the full entry call, including name lookup, argument binding, the exact
  function body, returns, frame popping, and the final result.

The theorem does not establish the result for other list lengths or when the
sole pair is not close. Those exclusions are why Gate B fails.

## Program boundary and observed state

`targetClosure` in `verification.k` macro-expands to the body generated in
`solution.mpy`: initialize `i`, `j`, and cached length `n`; execute the nested
loops; return early on a close pair; otherwise return `false`.

The entry claim begins with `Call(Name("has_close_elements"), ...)` in a module
scope containing that exact closure, so fixed semantics performs lookup,
argument evaluation and binding, frame creation, body execution, return, and
frame restoration. The observable final state is the returned Boolean. Internal
scope deallocation is existentially framed because it is not part of the
HumanEval result and the program does not mutate the input list.

## Proof-extension inventory

| Extension | Class | Role and domain | State/value effect | Justification and dependents |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `targetClosure` | Definitional summary | Compile-time macro for the exact function closure | No runtime rule; expands before proof | Direct expansion of `solution.mpy`; used by all three claims |
| `innerRun` | Definitional summary | Compile-time macro for the exact reachable inner-loop computation and continuation | No runtime rule or state abstraction | Syntactic abbreviation; used by `inner-loop-true` |
| `outerRun` | Definitional summary | Compile-time macro for the exact reachable outer-loop computation and continuation | No runtime rule or state abstraction | Syntactic abbreviation; used by `outer-loop-true` |

After macro expansion, `verification.k` adds no operational rule, function,
equation, simplification lemma, priority rule, totality axiom, or trusted claim.
All program operations execute with the supplied fixed semantics.

## Commands and actual results

The complete reproducible workflow is:

```bash
./prove.sh
```

Actual result: exit 0.

The positive proof commands run by that script were:

```bash
kprove spec.k --definition verification-kompiled \
  --spec-module SPEC --claims SPEC.inner-loop-true
kprove spec.k --definition verification-kompiled \
  --spec-module SPEC --claims SPEC.outer-loop-true
kprove spec.k --definition verification-kompiled \
  --spec-module SPEC --claims SPEC.entry-true
```

Each command printed `#Top` and exited 0.

Concrete LLVM execution used the required build:

```bash
